# .NET Build and Test Protocol

Run .NET verification from `{DOTNET}`. In Cursor, run it outside the sandbox: the sandbox blocks NuGet endpoints and can hang indefinitely at `Determining projects to restore...`.

Use `dotnet` on `PATH`. If `dotnet` is not found, use the path in `environment-resolver`.

## Guidance map

| When | Read |
|---|---|
| A dotnet build or test fails, hangs or leaves processes behind | rule `dotnet-build-failures` |

## Command constraints

- Never pipe or redirect `dotnet` output, except the one truncated-output rerun in rule `dotnet-build-failures`.
- Never pass verbosity flags.
- Do not build a large solution whole to verify a change; build the projects under Compilation check.
- Build after a coherent source change, before testing. Re-running the same tests without source changes may reuse `--no-build`.

## Compilation check

A new `git worktree` does not initialize submodules. If the repo has a `.gitmodules`, run `git -C <worktree> submodule update --init --recursive` first. A `CS0234` on a namespace the submodule provides, in a fresh worktree, is this environmental cause, not a source defect.

Build the smallest set of projects that covers the change: the host project that ships it, which compiles its upstream dependency chain, plus the test project that covers it, which catches downstream test compilation. Find them once per session:

```bash
rg -l 'Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web"' {DOTNET} -g '*.csproj'
rg -l 'Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk|<IsTestProject>true' {DOTNET} -g '*.csproj'
```

Pick the host and the test project that reference the changed project. A change that only a worker or console host ships builds that host instead.

```bash
dotnet build <HostProject>/<HostProject>.csproj
dotnet build <TestProject>/<TestProject>.csproj
```

A small solution can be built whole. A full-solution restore is allowed when packages are stale:

```bash
dotnet restore <Solution>.sln
```

## Impacted unit tests

Identify the test class corresponding to each changed service/class, then run against the fresh test build:

```bash
dotnet test <TestProject>/<TestProject>.csproj --no-build --filter "FullyQualifiedName~<TestClassName>"
```

Report pass/fail. If no impacted test exists, say so and ask whether one should be created. Do not claim completion from an earlier run that predates later source edits.
